th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/1a-Math-I-Calculus/punctured-neighbourhood.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1a-Math-I-Calculus/punctured-neighbourhood.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_punctured_neighbourhood_4c3ae978_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is a punctured neighbourhood of $x_0$?","options":["$\\{x\\in\\mathbb{R}\\colon|x-x_0| \\lt r\\}$ for some $r \\gt 0$.","$\\{x\\in\\mathbb{R}\\colon 0 \\lt |x-x_0| \\lt r\\}$ for some $r \\gt 0$.","$\\{x\\in\\mathbb{R}\\colon 0 \\lt |x-x_0| \\le r\\}$ for some $r \\gt 0$.","$\\{x\\in\\mathbb{R}\\colon|x-x_0| \\gt r\\}$ for some $r \\gt 0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"08c2eaff9b1d2f84313fa045","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_punctured_neighbourhood_4c3ae978_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does the condition $0 \\lt |x-x_0|$ ensure?","options":["The centre point $x_0$ is excluded.","Only the centre point $x_0$ is included.","The radius $r$ is negative.","The set contains no real numbers."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"27ceb319fb4b2633ce316d11","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_punctured_neighbourhood_4c3ae978_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which set is a punctured neighbourhood of $0$?","options":["$(-1,1)$.","$(-1,0)\\cup(0,1)$.","$[-1,0)\\cup(0,1]$.","$(-1,0)\\cup(0,2)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e2557838073a065c239922b6","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_punctured_neighbourhood_4c3ae978_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $x_0 = 2$ and $r = 1$, which point belongs to the punctured neighbourhood?","options":["$\\frac{5}{2}$.","$2$.","$3$.","$\\frac{1}{2}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c458c21bec04b2fc2bba30e3","time_limit":20},{"id":"punctured_neighbourhood_new_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does $|x-x_0| \\lt r$ express?","options":["$x$ is within distance $r$ of $x_0$","$x$ itself lies between $-r$ and $r$","$x$ is farther than $r$ from $x_0$","$x$ is within $r$ of $x_0$ and $x\\ne x_0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a4d44df5868735c8ab3be38e","time_limit":35},{"id":"punctured_neighbourhood_new_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which interval expression equals $\\{x\\in\\mathbb{R}\\colon 0 \\lt |x+1| \\lt 2\\}$?","options":["$(-1,1)\\cup(1,3)$","$(-3,1)$","$(-2,-1)\\cup(-1,0)$","$(-3,-1)\\cup(-1,1)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b3313754b1b5c708a006188d","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_punctured_neighbourhood_4c3ae978_005_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $f\\colon(0,1)\\to\\mathbb{R}$. Does the definition of $\\lim_{x\\to0}f(x)$ apply to this $f$?","options":["Yes: $0$ is a limit point of $(0,1)$, since every punctured neighbourhood of $0$ meets $(0,1)$.","No, because $0$ does not belong to the domain $(0,1)$.","No, because every punctured neighbourhood of $0$ contains negative numbers, where $f$ is undefined.","Yes, but only after a value $f(0)$ has been assigned."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d951a40bbec01ff0c2563cc2","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_punctured_neighbourhood_4c3ae978_012_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $X=[0,1]\\cup\\{3\\}$ and $f\\colon X\\to\\mathbb{R}$. Is $\\lim_{x\\to3}f(x)$ defined?","options":["No: $3$ is an isolated point of $X$, since $(2.5,3)\\cup(3,3.5)$ contains no point of $X$.","Yes, and it equals $f(3)$, because $3\\in X$.","Yes, because every point of a set is a limit point of that set.","Only the left-hand limit at $3$ is defined, because $X$ lies to the left of $3$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6dc09955eb30ffbaff47ee93","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_punctured_neighbourhood_4c3ae978_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $X=\\{\\frac{1}{n}\\colon n\\in\\mathbb{N},\\ n\\ge1\\}=\\{1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{3},\\dots\\}$. Which statement about limit points and isolated points of $X$ is correct?","options":["$0$ is not a limit point of $X$, because $0\\notin X$.","Every point of $X$ is a limit point of $X$, since the points of $X$ crowd together.","$0$ is a limit point of $X$, and every point of $X$ is an isolated point of $X$.","$0$ is an isolated point of $X$, because no point of $X$ lies to its left."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f7584bcbb6b666aff22ee544","time_limit":50}]}}